   "area_id": "ca_mt_gilbert",
   "name": "McGillicuddy",
   "grade": "5.10a",
   "discipline": "alpine",
   "overview": "A direct line up the pillar on Mount Gilbert's north face. Serious: loose rock abounds, especially below midheight. The roof pitch looks intimidating but is moderate. Eight pitches: loose scree ledges and a mossy chimney (5.7 to 5.7+), decomposing rock to a pair of towers called the Black Towers, a 4th-class scramble, then better granite with an arete crack pitch (5.8), two airy roofs (5.10a), moderate cracks to the pillar top, and a 4th-class plank to the summit ridge (stay roped). Descent: the 2nd-class southeast slope, across the bowl, to the East Couloir's upper notch, then rappel or downclimb the couloir (ice axe usually needed) and follow the valley to the Treasure Lakes path.",
