   "id": "ca_evolution_traverse",
   "area_id": "ca_mt_darwin",
   "name": "Evolution Traverse",
   "grade": "5.9",
   "discipline": "alpine",
   "overview": "Water is scarce; the nearest is a lake beneath Haeckel. Bivy sites are scarce too, though Darwin's summit is excellent and Haeckel offers some. Lengthy, occasionally intimidating; mostly excellent rock, a couple of loose spots. Mendel headwall (5.6) has a crux crack of high quality. The 13,332 to Darwin stretch is hardest in both technique and navigation: a manky downclimb graded anywhere from 5.6 to 5.9 sits under the summit block, and a brief rope-down sidesteps it; elsewhere, downclimbing and traversing reach 5.9. Huxley tops out around 5.7-5.8. Do not simulclimb. Leaving Darwin brings the greatest danger and difficulty: a rappel on poor anchors, or a downclimb at the same grade.",
